<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Saint Sava</h1><p class="st-meta">1448 CE – 1482 CE · capital Blagaj</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Domain of the Kosača family whose ruler's title, herceg, gave the region of Herzegovina its name.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stjepan-vukcic-kosaca">Stjepan Vukčić Kosača</a> inherited the lands of Hum and the coast from his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandalj-hranic-kosaca">Sandalj Hranić</a> in 1435, becoming the mightiest noble of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-bosnia">Kingdom of Bosnia</a>. In 1448 he styled himself Herceg of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-sava">Saint Sava</a>, after the relics of the Serbian saint kept at the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mileseva-monastery">Mileševa</a> in his territory, asserting independence from the Bosnian king.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>From his castle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blagaj">Blagaj</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mostar">Mostar</a>, Stjepan controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> between the Adriatic and the interior, levying customs on trade with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-ragusa">Ragusa</a> and developing the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herceg-novi">Novi</a>, today Herceg Novi. His lands mixed adherents of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnian-church">Bosnian Church</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and Catholic Christians, and he quarrelled with Ragusa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and his own son Vladislav.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> in 1463 and most of Stjepan's lands by 1465; he died in 1466. His son Vlatko held a shrinking coastal remnant until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herceg-novi">Herceg Novi</a> fell in 1482. Another son converted and became the Ottoman grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hersekzade-ahmed-pasha">Hersekzade Ahmed Pasha</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-saint-sava.jpg" alt="Duchy of Saint Sava" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_from_the_Kosaca_COA_crest.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1448 CE – 1482 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Blagaj</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Stjepan Vukčić Kosača, Vlatko Hercegović</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity (Bosnian Church, Orthodox, Catholic)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Serbo-Croatian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>, <a href="/countries/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Herzegovina (Kosača), Duchy of Herzegovina</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1465&amp;focus=duchy-of-saint-sava" aria-label="Duchy of Saint Sava on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5049 582 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Saint Sava lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5369 838L5437 871M5369 838L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5437 871h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5369 838h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5369 838h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Blagaj. <a href="/atlas?year=1465&amp;focus=duchy-of-saint-sava">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
